Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Sublet inside slabs.c and cache.c: the component port's lifetime hooks, opt-in
Upstream: memcached 1.6.45
Upstream-Archive: https://memcached.org/files/memcached-1.6.45.tar.gz
Upstream-SHA256: d362c64e6d8d5287153501eabf7c85b4a761432fbf53f5d7b085d0bb1653c1dd
Build-Variants: only with -DMC_CAPSTONE_SLAB_SUBLET (host/build-slab-sublet.sh); every other build is unchanged
Depends-On: none
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

The slab allocator never frees a chunk: do_slabs_free pushes an item on its class's slots list and
do_slabs_alloc pops it back, so an item is bounded to its 1 MiB page and nothing is ever revoked
(the safety fixtures 9 and 10 return on every heap arm). The allocators component port
(ports/memcached/allocators, patch 0002) hooks exactly those transitions; this patch carries the
same hooks, hunk for hunk, onto the application's own slabs.c and cache.c, without the component's
freestanding shims (its patch 0001), and calls the adapter's init before slabs_init in main.

Every hook is behind MC_CAPSTONE_SLAB_SUBLET with upstream's line in the #else, and calls a locked
wrapper (src/slab-sublet/mcapp-slab-sublet.c), never the adapter directly. host/build-slab-sublet.sh
checks that the hooks here are the component's, name for name and call for call.

--- a/slabs.c
